      "content": "i hear you on the fairness part, but if we're building this for a real bank, they're gonna want to know the actual statistical reason. if someone from Englewood has the exact same bank statement and job as someone from Lincoln Park, how does a model justify saying the Englewood person is a higher risk? isn't that just basically using the neighborhood to guess their race or social class, which is kind of the whole point of redlining? if the data says one thing but the ethics say another, how should the model actually prioritize that in a real-world risk calculation?",
